[flake8]
# WPS412 forbids logic in an __init__.py and WPS410 forbids module-level
# metadata such as __all__. Five package initializers deliberately carry
# import-time logic and are scoped exemptions here:
#
# * orchestrator/config/__init__.py invokes the `environment` resolver and
#   binds its results so reloading or patching `orchestrator.config` re-runs
#   resolution into the same namespace the helpers read -- the reload / patch
#   contract this package exists to preserve -- and publishes its narrow
#   public surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) in that initializer.
# * orchestrator/agents/__init__.py is the stable agent facade: it re-exports
#   the model types, the runner owner's `run_agent`, and the process owner's
#   `terminate_all_running`, and publishes its narrow public surface through an
#   explicit __all__ (WPS410) so `orchestrator.agents` stays the historical
#   import site. WPS412 is waived for that import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/github/__init__.py is the stable PyGithub client facade: it
#   re-exports the composed `GitHubClient` and the pinned durable-state model
#   from their owner modules, and publishes its narrow public surface through
#   an explicit __all__ (WPS410) so `orchestrator.github` stays the historical
#   import site. WPS412 is waived for that import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/scheduler/__init__.py is the stable scheduler facade: it
#   re-exports the concrete `IssueScheduler` from the `service` owner and the
#   caller-facing `SubmissionRequest` from the `models` owner, and publishes its
#   narrow public surface through an explicit __all__ (WPS410) so
#   `orchestrator.scheduler` stays the historical import site. WPS412 is waived
#   for that import-time logic.
# * orchestrator/workflow/__init__.py is the lazy workflow compatibility facade:
#   it installs the module-level `__getattr__` / `__dir__` hooks that resolve the
#   export manifest, so `orchestrator.workflow` stays the historical import site
#   and patch target for the whole inventory. WPS412 is waived for that
#   import-time logic; the `__all__` it publishes is resolved lazily through the
#   hooks rather than assigned in the initializer, so WPS410 does not apply.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/state.py is one owner for the typed workflow state --
# the label and control vocabularies, the transition graph keyed by them, and
# the coercion and guard every label write passes through -- so it carries 11
# members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7. Splitting the graph away from the labels
# it is keyed by, or the guard away from the graph it reads, would put the state
# machine back across the flat leaves this owner replaces, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/comments.py is one owner for both directions of
# the issue/PR comment surface -- the marker and id bookkeeping every posted
# comment goes through, and the trust-filtered read the agent prompts quote --
# so it carries 9 members past the same ceiling. The two directions are one
# policy: the marker the writer stamps is what the reader filters on, and
# splitting them would let the pair drift apart, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/messages.py is one owner for the whole agent
# message contract -- the markers read out of a last message and the stderr
# diagnostics written back when there is nothing usable in it -- so it carries
# 10 members past the same ceiling. Each marker is a small strict parser, and
# the prompt that promises one is the prompt that also promises the park the
# others fall to, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/prompts.py is one owner for the prompt builders
# the workflow stages share, so it carries 13 members past the same ceiling.
# They share one opening header and one set of notes, and each one's marker
# contract is what another owner then parses; splitting them by stage would let
# the prompt and the parser that reads its answer drift apart, so WPS202 is
# waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/usage.py is one owner for everything a tracked
# agent run is accounted by -- the audit pair and analytics record the spawn is
# bookended with, and the per-issue counters the usage that record parses is
# then folded into -- so it carries 10 members past the same ceiling and names
# 15 imports past the WPS201 ceiling of 12. The fold consumes the `UsageMetrics`
# the record produced, so splitting the halves apart would put one run's
# accounting across two modules, and the import list is just the union of what
# each half is typed by: the analytics sink and the agent result on one side,
# the issue, the pinned state, and the comment poster on the other. WPS201 and
# WPS202 are waived.
#
# orchestrator/workflow/engine/drift.py is one owner for what a user-content
# change is and what it costs -- the hash, the six filters that keep it from
# moving on content no human wrote, and the two routes a real move is handed
# to -- so it carries 9 members past the same ceiling. The routes are the only
# reason the hash exists, and a filter loosened without the route that fires on
# it is a re-decompose nobody asked for, so WPS202 is waived.
#
# orchestrator/agents/sessions.py is one owner for both JSONL parsing surfaces
# -- session-id extraction and Claude final-message extraction -- and each
# surface decomposes into small single-purpose helpers, so the module carries
# 11 members past the WPS202 ceiling of 7. orchestrator/agents/processes.py is
# one owner for the shared process registry and the subprocess-group lifecycle
# -- registration, bounded draining, timeout classification, and SIGTERM/SIGKILL
# escalation -- so it carries 9 members past the same ceiling.
# orchestrator/git/authentication.py owns everything the PAT travels through --
# per-repository token resolution, the askpass session and its detached
# environment, the two authenticated fetches, and the lease-pinned push -- so it
# carries 10 members past the same ceiling. Splitting any of them out would put
# a raw token on a module boundary. All three single-owner groupings are
# deliberate, so WPS202 is waived for those files.
#
# orchestrator/github/pull_requests.py owns the whole pull-request surface --
# branch/base lookup, creation, comments, labeling, retrieval, the SHA-pinned
# merge, and the head-branch delete -- so its client mixin carries 8 methods
# past the WPS214 ceiling of 7. Splitting the merge-side mutations out would
# hand the composed client two mixins for one owner, so WPS214 is waived here.
per-file-ignores =
    orchestrator/config/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/agents/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/github/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/scheduler/__init__.py:WPS412,WPS410
    orchestrator/workflow/__init__.py:WPS412
    orchestrator/workflow/state.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/workflow/engine/comments.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/workflow/engine/drift.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/workflow/engine/messages.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/workflow/engine/prompts.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/workflow/engine/usage.py:WPS201,WPS202
    orchestrator/agents/sessions.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/agents/processes.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/git/authentication.py:WPS202
    orchestrator/github/pull_requests.py:WPS214
